␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀# Copyright (C) 2011 Google Inc.  All Rights Reserved.
# Author: rws@google.com (Richard Sproat)
#
# Transformation from Welsh (cy) to its IPA transcription (cy_FONIPA).
# Based on description of Northern Welsh in:
#
# http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welsh_orthography
# http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welsh_phonology
#
# Note that these rules are NOT complete: to be complete we would have to know
# the morphological analysis of the word. For example, final <au> is pronounced
# /a/ if it is the noun plural marker, otherwise it is /aÉ¨/. Similarly in
# "llongyfarch" ('congratulating'), the morphological decomposition --- "llon +
# cyfarch" --- is needed to know that the <ng> is pronounced as /Å‹g/, not as
# /Å‹/.

::Lower;
::NFC;

## Class definitions

$end = [$ ];
## Both orthographic and phonetic vowels
$vowel = [aeiouwyÃ¢ÃªÃ®Ã´Ã»ÅµÅ·É‘É¨É™É›ÉªÊŠ];
## W is a placeholder for the glide -- see below
$cons = [Å‹ÌŠÏ‡Ã°É¬mÌ¥nÌ¥Å‹rÌ¥Î¸bkdvfghlmnprstÊƒWjÊ’];

## Preprocessing of letters that sometimes occur

k â†’ c;
v â†’ f;
x â†’ s;
z â†’ s;

::Null;

## Consonant transductions:

## Trigraphs

ngh â†’ Å‹ÌŠ;

## Digraphs

ch â†’ Ï‡;
dd â†’ Ã°;
ff â†’ F;  # Temporary register
ll â†’ É¬;
mh â†’ mÌ¥;
nh â†’ nÌ¥;
ng â†’ Å‹;
ph â†’ F;  # Temporary register
rh â†’ rÌ¥;
th â†’ Î¸;

## Monographs

b â†’ b;
c â†’ k;
d â†’ d;
f â†’ v;
g â†’ g;
h â†’ h;
j â†’ dÊ’;   ## Loan words
l â†’ l;
m â†’ m;
n â†’ n;
p â†’ p;
r â†’ r;
s â†’ s;
t â†’ t;

::Null;

F â†’ f;

## Transduce <si> to /Êƒ/ before vowels

si} $vowel â†’ Êƒ;

::Null;

## Treatment of glides.

## First transduce <i>, <w> to glides prior to vowels. With <w> we want to
## do this also before /r,l/ after /g/ (<Proto-Celtic *w) e.g. "gwlad",
## "gwraig". However the "after g" environment must allow for the following
## possibilities:
##
## g -> Å‹ via nasal mutation
## g -> 0 via soft mutation

{i} $vowel â†’ j;
{w} $vowel â†’ W; # Temporary register
[gÅ‹] {w} [rl] $vowel â†’ W;   ## Plain or nasal mutation environment
^ {w} [rl] $vowel â†’ W;      ## Soft mutation at the beginning of a word

## Transduce accented <áºƒ> to <w>: this is used to indicate when a <w> that would
## normally be expected to be a glide, is instead a vowel:

áºƒ â†’ w;

::Null;

## Stress placement, needed for vowel quality/quantity prediction Basic rule of
## stress in Welsh is to place it on the penult, except of course in
## monosyllables.

} $vowel+ $cons+ $vowel+ $cons* $end â†’ Ëˆ;  ## Polysyllabic words

^ $cons* {} $vowel+ $cons* $end â†’ Ëˆ;       ## Monosyllabic words

::Null;

## Transduction of vowels

## The first rule above overgenerates streams of stress marks. The rule below
## cleans that up.

Ëˆ+ â†’ Ëˆ;

## Diphthongs

## Deal with <y> first since we also need to lengthen the /É¨/ if that is in the
## correct environment for lengthening.

## <y> is /É¨/ in final syllable, otherwise /É™/

yw } $cons* $end â†’ É¨u;
yw â†’ É™u;

y} $cons* $end â†’ É¨;
y â†’ É™;

::Null;

## Diphthongs in long environment

## Final, or before word-final s

Ëˆ { É¨u } s? $end â†’ É¨Ëu;
Ëˆ { aw } s? $end â†’ É‘Ëu;
Ëˆ { ew } s? $end â†’ eËu;
Ëˆ { oe } s? $end â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { ou } s? $end â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { wy } s? $end â†’ uËÉ¨;

# before b, ch, d, dd, g, f, ff, th followed by the end of a word
# or a vowel

Ëˆ { É¨u } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ É¨Ëu;
Ëˆ { aw } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ É‘Ëu;
Ëˆ { ew } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ eËu;
Ëˆ { oe } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { ou } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { wy } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $end â†’ uËÉ¨;

Ëˆ { É¨u } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ É¨Ëu;
Ëˆ { aw } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ É‘Ëu;
Ëˆ { ew } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ eËu;
Ëˆ { oe } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { ou } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ É”ËÉ¨;
Ëˆ { wy } [bÏ‡dÃ°gvfÎ¸] $vowel â†’ uËÉ¨;

## Diphthongs in other environments

ae â†’ É‘ËÉ¨;
ai â†’ ai;
au â†’ aÉ¨; ## As plural ending /a/, but we can't predict this
aw â†’ au;
ei â†’ É™i;
